{"builder":"Goal: one pull request that adds clamp(x, lo, hi) to mathlib (issue https://github.com/o/r/issues/23) and meets every one of these lines:\n- math.js exports clamp(x, lo, hi), which returns x limited to the range lo to hi\n- clamp(x, lo, hi) throws a RangeError when lo is greater than hi\n- README.md documents every function math.js exports\n- npm test passes\n\nWhat the repo has now: math.js (CommonJS, 'use strict') defines add, subtract and multiply and ends with `module.exports = { add, subtract, multiply };`. README.md has an `## API` section with `### add(a, b)` and `### multiply(a, b)` entries. **subtract is exported but not in the README.** Tests are in test/math.test.js and run with node:test (`npm test` runs `node --test`).\n\nSteps:\n1. In math.js add `function clamp(x, lo, hi)` with a short doc comment like the other functions. If lo > hi, throw `new RangeError(...)` with a clear message (for example `clamp: lo (${lo}) must not be greater than hi (${hi})`). Otherwise return lo when x < lo, hi when x > hi, and x otherwise (`Math.min(Math.max(x, lo), hi)` works once the range has been checked). lo === hi is a valid range and returns lo. Add clamp to module.exports, and keep add, subtract and multiply exported.\n2. In README.md, under `## API`, add a `### subtract(a, b)` entry (it returns `a - b`) and a `### clamp(x, lo, hi)` entry. Follow the style of the existing entries: a heading with the signature, one sentence, and a short ```js example. The clamp entry must say that it returns x limited to [lo, hi] and that it throws a RangeError when lo is greater than hi. Give examples such as `clamp(-5, 0, 100); // 0`, `clamp(140, 0, 100); // 100` and `clamp(42, 0, 100); // 42`. When you are done, every name in math.js's module.exports must have its own `### name(` heading in README.md.\n3. In test/math.test.js, import clamp and add tests for: a value below the range (gives lo), a value above it (gives hi), a value inside it (gives x), the boundaries (x === lo, x === hi), lo === hi, and `assert.throws(() => clamp(5, 10, 0), RangeError)`.\n4. Run `npm test` and make sure it exits 0 with no failing tests. Then commit and open the pull request, referencing issue #23.","verifier":"Check out the pull request head and run every check there. Run `npm install` first only if package.json now lists dependencies. There is one check for each expect line of issue https://github.com/o/r/issues/23, in order.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"math.js exports clamp(x, lo, hi), which returns x limited to the range lo to hi\"\nRun from the repository root:\n`node -e \"const m=require('./math.js'); const c=m.clamp; console.log(typeof c, c(-5,0,100), c(140,0,100), c(42,0,100), c(0,0,100), c(100,0,100), c(7,3,3), c(-2.5,-10,-1))\"`\nPASS: it prints `function 0 100 42 0 100 3 -2.5`. That means clamp is exported from math.js, values below the range give lo, values above give hi, values inside (including the boundaries) are returned unchanged, and lo === hi gives that value.\nFAIL: clamp is missing or not a function, the command throws for any of these valid ranges, or any printed value differs.\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"clamp(x, lo, hi) throws a RangeError when lo is greater than hi\"\nRun:\n`node -e \"const {clamp}=require('./math.js'); for (const a of [[5,10,0],[0,1,-1],[50,100,0]]) { try { const r=clamp(...a); console.log('NO THROW', a, r); } catch(e){ console.log(e instanceof RangeError ? 'RangeError' : 'OTHER '+(e&&e.name)); } }\"`\nPASS: all three lines print `RangeError`.\nFAIL: any line prints `NO THROW` or `OTHER ...`.\n\nCheck 3. Expect line: \"README.md documents every function math.js exports\"\nRun `node -e \"console.log(Object.keys(require('./math.js')).filter(k=>typeof require('./math.js')[k]==='function').join(' '))\"` to list the exported functions. Then read README.md at the head.\nPASS: README.md documents every listed function, each one by name with at least a description of what it does. At minimum this includes add, subtract, multiply and clamp. Note that subtract was not documented before this change.\nFAIL: any exported function is not documented in README.md.\n\nCheck 4. Expect line: \"npm test passes\"\nRun `npm test` from the repository root.\nPASS: the run reports no failing tests and the command exits 0.\nFAIL: any test fails, or the command exits non-zero.\n\nAdvisory (not pass/fail conditions):\n- Check that test/math.test.js has new tests for clamp, including the RangeError case.\n- Check that the README clamp entry mentions the RangeError, and that each function has its own `### name(` heading under `## API` like the existing entries.\n- Check that the RangeError message is meaningful."}